{"kind":"artifact","text":"Deployment note repaired at exact head 347baa0332108ec99532649db9c95bac560e34b1, against round-10 report 19fc2724dda78a65d72231e94caacaa38e32904d. Worktree clean, whitespace clean.\n\nYou are right, and I reproduced it before repairing rather than taking the probe on trust. My first attempt at that reproduction showed the refused candidate retained under both topologies, which would have made your finding look wrong. It was my harness: I had deleted the inbox ref but left the original branch pointing at the candidate, so gc had nothing to collect. Removing every other ref so the ledger is the only possible retainer gives your result exactly — sole-parent prunes the candidate, candidate-parent retains it.\n\nThe error is worth naming plainly because it is the third instance of one class in this file. \"An all-refused batch has no candidates\" is false: refused is a verdict about a candidate, not the absence of one, and every refused inbox branch points at a submitter-authored commit that nothing else retains once the drain deletes that ref. The batch note kept identifiers and lost objects; this kept the refusal line and lost the object it describes. In both cases the record still reads correctly after the loss, which is exactly why it survived a round of review — mine and yours.\n\nWhat makes it worse than a retention bug is that it falsified a claim made in the same commit that introduced it. I wrote that the payload lets an auditor reconcile a batch without trusting the drain, and simultaneously specified the topology that makes it impossible: no candidate object, so no recomputed identity, no recheckable reason, no testable one-line-per-submission property. Only the drain's own summary of what it discarded. I introduced the mechanism and removed its evidence in one edit.\n\nRepaired as you specify. E stays P and is still not a parent, being already reachable. The all-refused entry is parented on the previous ledger head and every refused candidate. The general ledger rule now says every candidate the drain considered, admitted and refused alike, rather than leaving \"every candidate\" to be read as admitted-only — that phrasing is what let the narrower reading in.\n\nThe bijection is stated with each half independently falsifiable: every candidate parent has exactly one payload line and every line names one parent by hash; an admitted line's event commit lies on P..E; a refused line's reason is recomputable by re-running admission against the retained candidate. That last clause is the one that needs the parents, and it is what turns \"without trusting the drain\" into something an auditor can fail rather than a phrase.\n\nSpike case 4 asserted the old one-parent shape, so it followed. It now also spells out the prune sequence — delete both inbox refs, expire reflogs, gc --prune=now, then require cat-file -e to succeed and both reasons to recompute. Without that sequence the case passes with no parents at all, since the inbox refs still hold the objects. That is the false green that made the sole-parent rule look correct in the first place, so leaving the case to imply it would have preserved the defect in executable form.","body":{"commit":"347baa0332108ec99532649db9c95bac560e34b1","path":"notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md"}}